Hi Astound Commerce team,

We sent you a client security audit for underarmour.com with a published grad=
e of F (35/100). That grade was wrong. Our scoring model at the time penalise=
d every finding flatly and counted best-practice configuration items as vulne=
rabilities. Re-scoring the same scan under the corrected model gives C (70/10=
0).

Three finding classes drove the wrong grade: the SSL/TLS certificate expiring=
 soon, the missing HTTP response hardening headers (CSP, X-Frame-Options, X-C=
ontent-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy, COOP) and the DMARC=
 policy in inactive monitoring mode (p=3Dnone).

One critical or high finding in that report is unaffected by this correction =
and still needs attention.

The rule now: best-practice items are reported but never scored, a scan with =
no critical or high finding cannot score below 60, and repeated findings of t=
he same class are charged at a reduced rate under a per-class cap.

Next step: reply to this note and we will re-issue the underarmour.com audit =
with the corrected grade and score. No change to the client's configuration i=
s required to correct the score.

Best regards,
Carson Haynes
Founder, Orbit Security
carsonmail009@gmail.com
